            {
               "text": "### Images from ESA\n\nThese images are copyright for ESA and allowed to be used according to the license determined by them.\n\nTo visualize all images, use the left and right arrows on the slide below.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div class=\"htSlides\"><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">1 / 5</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgESA2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgESA2', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Planck, The History of the Universe . ©ESA – C. Carreau (<htcite0>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Origin of the universe - Our current understanding of the evolution of the universe and the view people had of it millennia ago. (<htcite2>)</li><li>All together - An initial explanation of the period when matter and energy were united. (<htcite3>)</li><li>Limits and contexts - a common point between science and texts is the fact that people do not always understand their limits. But how to identify them? (<htcite4>)</li><li>Building to count (Suanpan, 算盤) - In this text, we see the differences and what the Chinese and Japanese abacuses have in common. (<htcite5>)</li><li>Is the universe static? - In this text, we take a trip through time to understand what current measurements reveal about the past of the universe. (<htcite6>)</li><li>Expanding... - Continuing from the previous text, we see that dividing into only two sides ends up omitting the details (<htcite12>).</li><li>Two sides - good vs. evil, left vs. right, matter vs. energy, up vs. down. Is this duality sufficient? (<htcite9>)</li><li>Evolution of knowledge: The atom - In this text, we study how our idea of the atom evolved over time. (<htcite10>)</li><li>Together we are strong - In this text, we see how the four operations, together, open the doors to much knowledge. (<htcite11>)</li><li>Where are they?: In this last text of the chapter, we learn where we can find the numbers studied throughout the chapter (<htcite13>).</li><li>Shared Origin: After observing different ways of grouping and counting quantities, we move on to studying the sharing of these quantities (<htcite14>).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">2 / 5</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgESA1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgESA1', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">View of the Cosmic Microwave Background (CMB) through the ESA Planck mission. ©ESA/Planck Collaboration (<htcite1>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Cosmic Microwave Background - An explanation of how the Cosmic Microwave Background helped confirm the Big Bang theory. (<htcite7>)</li><li>Is the universe static? - In this text, we take a trip through time to understand what current measurements reveal about the past of the universe. (<htcite15>)</li><li>Shared Origin: After observing different ways of grouping and counting quantities, we move on to studying the sharing of these quantities (<htcite8>)</li><li>Texts with Sciences (Part I): In the initial texts, we analyze the relationship between texts and various sciences, although we have not yet performed a complete textual interpretation. In this text, we begin the exploration of ancient Mesopotamian texts, using the support of scientific studies (<htcite16>).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">3 / 5</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgUniverseTimeBigBang\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgUniverseTimeBigBang', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">The universe through space-time.  ©ESA (<htcite17>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>Cosmic Inflation: How the idea of cosmic inflation helped solve a problem with the Big Bang theory (<htcite18>).</li><li>The beginning: Marking time requires a beginning. But what would it be? (<htcite19>).</li><li>The Big Bang: A bit of the history of the Big Bang theory (<htcite20>).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">4 / 5</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgWebbDisc\"onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgWebbDisc', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Disk of gas and dust around a young star. ©ESA/Webb, NASA & CSA, M. Villenave et al. (<htcite21>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>How is a Planet formed?: We explain the formation of a planet (<htcite22>).</li></ul></div></div><div class=\"htSlide\"><div class=\"htSlideCounter\">5 / 5</div><img class=\"imgGameSize\" id=\"imgGaiaStarFormation\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgGaiaStarFormation', '0%')\"><div class=\"htSlideCaption\">Star-forming regions in the Milky Way. ©ESA/Gaia/DPAC, S. Payne-Wardenaar, L. McCallum et al (<htcite23>).</div><div class=\"htSlideRefs\"><b>Related content:</b><ul><li>The Birth of a Star: We explain the stages of the birth of a star (<htcite24>).</li></ul></div></div><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-left htSlidePrev\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(-1);\"></i><i class=\"fa-solid fa-chevron-right htSlideNext\" onclick=\"htPlusDivs(1);\"></i></div></p>",
